          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            Generally, the term
            %em Mackey functor
            refers to an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/additive+functor"} additive functor
            from (a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subcategory"} subcategory
            of) a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+correspondences"} category of correspondences
            (in a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/disjunctive+category"} disjunctive category
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/additive+category"} additive category
            which however usually is the “base” abelian category. More generally the term now refers to the fairly obvious
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theoretic
            or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+category+theory"} higher categorical
            refinements of this concept.
          %p
            Therefore the concept of Mackey functors is similar to that of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sheaves+with+transfer"} sheaves with transfer
            and as such appears (implicitly) in the discussion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/motives"} motives
            (explicitly e.g. in
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#KahnYamazaki11"} Kahn-Yamazaki 11, section 2
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            is a category of suitable
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/schemes"} schemes
            %a{:href => "#Weibel05"} this article of Weibel
            and Voevodsky’s seminal article defining his triangulated category of motives, specifically
            %a{:href => "#Voevodsky00"} Section 3.4
            about geometrical 0-motives).
          %p
            Specifically, the concept was introduced and named as such in the context of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representation+theory"} representation theory
            (
            %a{:href => "#Dress71"}> Dress 71
            ) and
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivariant+homotopy+theory"} equivariant homotopy theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivariant+cohomology"} equivariant cohomology
            (
            %a{:href => "#May96"}> May 96
            ). Here the underlying
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+correspondences"} category of correspondences
            is that in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+set"} finite
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/G-sets"} G-sets
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Burnside+category"} Burnside category
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivariant+homotopy+groups"} equivariant homotopy groups
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi E
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_n(E)
            of a (genuine)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/G-spectrum"} G-spectrum
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            organize into a Mackey functor on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Burnside+category"} Burnside category
            with values in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+groups"} abelian groups
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivariant+Whitehead+theorem"} equivariant Whitehead theorem
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/G-spectra"} G-spectra
            themselves are equivalent to Mackey
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-functors"} (∞,1)-functors
            from the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Burnside+category"} Burnside category
            to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category+of+spectra"} (∞,1)-category of spectra
            (
            %a{:href => "#GuillouMay11"}> GuillouMay 11
            ,
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Barwick14"} Barwick 14
          %h2#definition Definition
          %h3#in_higher_category_theory In higher category theory
          %p
            We follow the account in (
            %a{:href => "#Barwick14"}> Barwick 14
            ), which incarnates a perspective of (
            %a{:href => "#Lindner76"}> Lindner 76
            ) in higher category theory.
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            be a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/disjunctive+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} disjunctive (∞,1)-category
            and write
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Span
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝒞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Span(\mathcal{C})
            for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category+of+correspondences"} (∞,1)-category of correspondences
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            and let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒜
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{A}
            be an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} (∞,1)-category
            with finite products.
          %p
            Then an
            %em
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒜
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{A}
              \-valued semi-Mackey functor
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            is a product-preserving functor
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Span
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝒞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⟶
                  %mi 𝒜
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  M \;\colon\; \Span(\mathcal{C}) \longrightarrow \mathcal{A}
                  \\,.
          %p
            If
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒜
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{A}
            is additive, we say that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            is a
            = succeed "." do
              %strong Mackey functor
          %p
            Notice that this means that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            is in particular:
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                a covariant
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-functor"} (∞,1)-functor
                = succeed ";" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %msub
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo *
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                        %mi 𝒞
                        %mo →
                        %mi 𝒜
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (-)_\ast \colon\mathcal{C} \to \mathcal{A}
            %li
              %p
                a contravariant
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-functor"} (∞,1)-functor
                = succeed ";" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %msup
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo *
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                        %msup
                          %mi 𝒞
                          %mi op
                        %mo →
                        %mi 𝒜
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (-)^\ast \colon\mathcal{C}^{op} \to \mathcal{A}
            %li
              %p
                satisfy the
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Beck-Chevalley+condition"} Beck-Chevalley condition
          %p
            In order to understand the Beck-Chevalley condition, note that disjunctiveness of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            implies that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Span
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝒞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Span(\mathcal{C})
            is
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semiadditive+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} semiadditive
          %p
            (More generally one may specify suitably chosen sub-
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn ∞
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
            \-categories
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi 𝒞
                    %mo †
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝒞
                    %mo †
                  %mo ⊂
                  %mi 𝒞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}^\dagger, \mathcal{C}_\dagger \subset \mathcal{C}
            and restrict
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Span
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝒞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Span(\mathcal{C})
            to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/correspondences"} correspondences
            whose left leg is in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝒞
                    %mo †
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}_\dagger
            and whose right leg is in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi 𝒞
                    %mo †
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}^\dagger
            (
            %a{:href => "#Barwick14"}> Barwick 14, section 5
            ).)
          %h3#in_representation_theory In representation theory
          %p
            In order to recover the original definition of
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Dress71"} Dress 71
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            a finite group, and we let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝔽
                    %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{F}_G
            be the (disjunctive) category of finite
            = succeed "-sets." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            by defining
            = succeed "-Mackey" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Span
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝔽
                    %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⟶
                  %mi 𝒜
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  M \;\colon\; \Span(\mathbb{F}_G) \longrightarrow \mathcal{A}
                  \\,.
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Span
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝔽
                    %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Span(\mathbb{F}_G)
            has
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/biproducts"} biproducts
            given by disjoint unions of finite
            = succeed "-sets;" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒜
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{A}
            is a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semiadditive+category"} semiadditive category
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                a covariant functor
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %msub
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo *
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %msub
                        %mi 𝒪
                        %mi G
                      %mo →
                      %mi 𝒜
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (-)_\ast \colon \mathcal{O}_G \to \mathcal{A}
                and
            %li
              %p
                a contravariant functor
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %msup
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo *
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                        %msubsup
                          %mi 𝒪
                          %mi G
                          %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} op
                        %mo →
                        %mi 𝒜
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (-)^\ast \colon \mathcal{O}_G^{\op} \to \mathcal{A}
            %li
              %p
                there exists a non-natural isomorphism
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %msub
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo *
                        %mo ≃
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %msup
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo *
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (-)_\ast \simeq (-)^\ast
            %li
              %p
                writing
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msubsup
                        %mi R
                        %mi J
                        %mi H
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R^H_J
                for the contravariant effect of in inclusion
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi J
                      %mo ⊂
                      %mi H
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J \subset H
                and
