                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi H
                  %mo ↦
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %msub
                    %mi H
                    %mo +
                  %mo ∧
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mi n
                  %mo ,
                  %mi X
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mi G
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\pi_n(E) \colon G/H \mapsto [G/H_+\wedge S^n, X]_G
                  \\,,
          %p
            where now on the right we have just the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+classes"} homotopy classes
            of maps, i.e. the morphisms in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivariant+stable+homotopy+category"} equivariant stable homotopy category
            (e.g.
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#GreenleesMay95"} Greenlees-May 95, p. 43
          %p (…)
          %h3#group_cohomology Group (co)homology
          %p
            Given
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            a
            = succeed "-module," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℤ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi G
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}[G]
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi H
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi G
                    %mo ,
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H^n(G,U)
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi H
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi G
                    %mo ,
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H_n(G,U)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mover
                      %mi H
                      %mo ^
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi G
                  %mo ,
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \widehat H^n(G,U)
            form Mackey functors under the usual restriction and transfers (or corestriction). See example 53.3 of
            %a{:href => "#Thevenaz"} Thévenaz
          %h3#ideal_class_groups Ideal class groups
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            be a field with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E/F
            a finite Galois extension and write
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                    %mo ≔
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Gal
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi E
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G \coloneqq \mathrm{Gal}(E/F)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Cl
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi E
                    %mi H
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{Cl}(E^H)
            for the ideal class group of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi E
                      %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E^H
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Cl
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi E
                    %mi H
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Cl
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi E
                    %mi K
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{Cl}(E^H) \rightarrow \mathrm{Cl}(E^K)
            and norm maps yield a map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Cl
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi E
                    %mi K
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Cl
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi E
                    %mi H
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{Cl}(E^K) \rightarrow \mathrm{Cl}(E^H)
            satisfying a double coset formula; together, these form a Mackey functor. See example 53.12 of
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Thevenaz"} Thévenaz
          %h2#Cohomology Cohomology with coefficients in a Mackey functor
          %p
            We discuss
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology"} cohomology
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+G-spaces"} topological G-spaces
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coefficients"} coefficients
            in a Mackey functor, following notation and conventions as in (
            %a{:href => "#May96"}> May 96, sections IX, X
            ). See also (
            %a{:href => "#GreenleesMay95"}> Greenlees-May 95, p. 9
            ).
          #CohomologyOfGSpace.num_defn
            %h6#defininition Defininition
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pointed+object"} pointed
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/G-CW+complex"} G-CW complex
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chain+complex"} chain complex
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi C
                      %mo •
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_\bullet(X)
              of Mackey functors to be given by the stable
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivariant+homotopy+groups"} equivariant homotopy groups
              of the quotient spaces
              = succeed ":" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi X
                        %mo •
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %msup
                        %mi X
                        %mrow
                          %mo •
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                          %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X^{\bullet}/X^{\bullet-1}
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi C
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≔
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi X
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %msup
                      %mi X
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo ,
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    C_n(X) \coloneqq \pi_n(X^n/X^{n-1})
                    \\,,
            %p
              Then for
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              any Mackey functor, the ordinary cohomology of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              with
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coefficients"} coefficients
              in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              is the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cochain+cohomology"} cochain cohomology
              of the complex of homs of Mackey functors
              = succeed ":" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi C
                        %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo →
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_n(X) \to A
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mi H
                      %mi G
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≔
                    %msup
                      %mi H
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Hom
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi C
                      %mo •
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    H_G^n(X,A) \coloneqq H^n( Hom(C_\bullet(X), A) )
                    \\,.
            %p
              More generally, for
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
              a G-
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representation"}> representation
              , the
              = succeed "-" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi n
                      %mo −
                      %mi V
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n-V)
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/RO%28G%29-grading"} RO(G)-graded
              cohomology of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              with coefficients in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              is
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mi H
                      %mi G
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo −
                        %mi V
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %msubsup
                      %mi H
                      %mi G
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mi V
                    %mo ∧
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    H_G^{n-V}(X,A) = H_G^n(S^V \wedge X,A)
                    \\,.
          %p
            (
            %a{:href => "#May96"}> May 96, section X.4 def. 4.1, def. 4.2
            )
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark Remark
            %p
              The corresponding
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reduced+cohomology"} reduced cohomology
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mover
                        %mi H
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo ,
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tilde H^n(-,A)
              is represented by maps into the
              = succeed ":" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eilenberg-MacLane+space"} Eilenberg-MacLane G-space
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mover
                        %mi H
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≃
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi K
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo ,
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mi G
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\tilde H^n(X,A) \simeq [X,K(A,n)]_G
                    \\,.
          %p
            (
            %a{:href => "#GreenleesMay95"}> Greenlees-May 95
            )
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_2 Remark
            %p
              For this kind of cohomology, there is equivariant
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Serre+spectral+sequence"} Serre spectral sequence
              (
              %a{:href => "#Kronholm10"}> Kronholm 10
              ).
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tambara+functor"} Tambara functor
          %h2#references References
          %h3#plain_mackey_functors Plain Mackey functors
          %p The original article is
          %ul
            %li#Dress71
              A. W. M. Dress,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Notes on the theory of representations of finite groups. Part I: The Burnside ring of a finite group and some AGN-applications, Bielefeld
          %p The span perspective is due to
          %ul
            %li#Lindner76
